    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Yamaha Births New Adventure Scooter Family, Three RW Models That Look More Like Motorcycles\n1966 Chevy Impala Sitting on Private Property Begs for Restoration With a Big Block on the Outside\n29 photos\nPhoto: eBay seller tolu-988\nA 1966 Chevrolet Impala is certainly worth checking out if you are in the market for a restoration project, and this specimen is trying to convince the audience with a big-block engine. However, you don’t need to be a diehard restoration guy to figure out the obvious.\nThis isn’t an easy project, and whoever buys the car will have to do extensive work in all key areas, including the engine.\nThe big block I told you about is on the outside, as it has already been removed from the car, probably to be rebuilt. It hasn’t been rebuilt, so the only thing the current owner did was to remove the engine, and that’s all.\nPhoto: eBay seller tolu-988 1966 was such a big year for the Impala. America’s best-selling car was now all about big blocks, and the Super Sport was more powerful than ever.\nThis was the second year of a new generation that started in 1965 and whose sales went through the roof. The 1965 Impala was the first car in the United States to sell more than 1 million units in one year, and Chevrolet hoped the 1966 model year would do the same.\nHowever, some of the changes the GM brand operated in the Impala lineup prevented the Impala from reaching the same numbers, despite the full-size series still selling like hotcakes. The Caprice was now a stand-alone series - no longer being part of the Impala lineup - so on paper, the Impala looked less successful - it wasn’t.\nChevrolet ended the year with over 2.2 million cars sold to American buyers, and the full-size lineup accounted for nearly 1.5 million units. The Caprice was a highly desirable model, with 181,000 orders. The Super Sport was also a separate series with coupe and convertible body styles, representing 119,300 orders.\nThe engine lineup available for the 1966 model year was impressive, to say the least. The L35 396 continued to be offered with 325 horsepower, but this time, buyers who wanted more power could order a 427 big-block engine with 390 horsepower. This was the L36 and carried an extra price tag of $181.\nThe top engine that could be installed on the Impala was the famous L72, which made its way to only $1,858 cars. It produced 425 horsepower but could be had for $312.\nPhoto: eBay seller tolu-988 This Impala comes with a 396, but it’s no longer under the hood. This isn’t necessarily a problem, but it’s unclear if this is the original factory engine.\nThe owner explains in the eBay post that the car is no longer original, so my recommendation if you’re specifically interested in this part is to call the seller and discuss the rest of the details. Sure, nothing beats a live inspection, but a walkaround video would also come in very handy.\nThe photos suggest this Impala has been sitting for quite some time, and the dirty interior confirms this is likely the case. The cabin looks good though, and despite being dirty and needing thorough cleaning, it looks to be in decent shape. No big parts appear to be missing, and that’s good news for someone who plans a complete restoration.\nPhoto: eBay seller tolu-988 The Impala requires rust repairs, but despite the obvious problems that you can see in the photos, it still has a clean frame. You will need a good restoration guy to fix everything on this Impala, but considering you’ll also get lots of extra parts, there’s a chance that putting the car back together and giving it a second chance won’t require as much effort as it looks at first glance.\nRestoring a 1966 Impala can be worth the time and money if you’re specifically planning to sell it for profit, though I think the biggest shortcoming of this specimen is the non-SS setup. Collectors would pay big bucks for a 1966 Impala SS, especially with a 427 under the hood or sold separately (if original).\nThis doesn’t mean this non-SS doesn’t deserve our attention. It does, and there’s no doubt it would make an excellent showstopper if restored correctly.\nPhoto: eBay seller tolu-988 It’ll certainly be interesting to keep tabs on the last few minutes of this auction. First, this is a no-reserve auction, so the highest bidder will take it home when the battle ends. Second, nobody has submitted an offer so far, but this isn’t necessarily a surprise, as many potential buyers are waiting for the final seconds specifically to avoid the fierce competition that typically emerges during a no-reserve auction.\nThe Impala costs at least $5,000 - unless someone submits a higher offer - and the auction will end in just a few hours. The car is parked in Anoka, Minnesota, and will also require transportation, considering it doesn’t move on its own. Just make sure you have enough space to also get the engine and the extras included in the sale.\nVia // eBay\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nImpala Chevrolet Chevrolet Impala 1966 Impala WEEKEND\nAbout the author: Bogdan Popa\nBogdan keeps an eye on how technology is taking over the car world. His long-term goals are buying an 18-wheeler because he needs more space for his kid’s toys, and convincing Google and Apple that Android Auto and CarPlay deserve at least as much attention as their phones. Full profile\nUP NEXT 2028 Toyota 4Runner Gets a Mid-Cycle Refresh Inside and Outside, Though Only Across Imagination Land",
